<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alabama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alabama is a state in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> River valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> at Mobile. It was home to Mississippian chiefdoms such as Moundville and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a>, Choctaw and Chickasaw, most of whom were forcibly removed in the 1830s. Admitted to the Union in 1819, it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>-growing slave state, a founding member of the Confederacy and later a centre of the civil rights movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alabama.jpg" alt="Alabama" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve Hall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Alabama.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
